{#
/**
 * @file
 * Theme override to display a one-column layout.
 *
 * Available variables:
 * - in_preview: Whether the plugin is being rendered in preview mode.
 * - content: The content for this layout.
 * - attributes: HTML attributes for the layout <div>.
 * - settings: The section configuration, including the selected theme (see
 *   \Drupal\ys_layouts\Plugin\Layout\YSLayoutOneColumn).
 *
 * Renders through `@organisms/layout/layout` (`yds-layout.twig`) exactly like
 * Two Column (70/30), Two Column (50/50) and Three Column (33/33/33), so all
 * four section layouts emit ONE signature from ONE template:
 *
 *   <div class="yds-layout layout" data-component-theme="X"
 *        data-component-layout="Y" …>
 *
 * `component__layout: 'one-column'` is what tells the organism this is a
 * single-region layout, so it renders the content directly in the section
 * element instead of wrapping it in the `__inner` flex row and a `__primary`
 * column. Adding a new section type (a 30/70, say) means a new
 * `component__layout` value there, not a new template here.
 *
 * This layout used to hand-roll the `yds-layout` class and the
 * `data-component-theme` attribute itself, and only when a theme was picked.
 * It now always renders through the organism (YaleSites-Internal#1613), so an
 * unthemed section carries `data-component-theme="default"` where it
 * previously carried no section markup at all. Two rules exist because of
 * that, and both are load-bearing:
 *  - `_yds-layout.scss` excludes `one-column` from the `[data-component-theme=
 *    'default']` section-margin rule. One column's page-level margins come
 *    from `.main-content > *:first-child` (page-layouts.scss) and
 *    `.main-content .layout.layout--onecol:last-of-type` (layout-builder.css),
 *    so applying section margins would double them on every page.
 *  - `ys_layouts/layouts/onecol/onecol.css` scopes its vertical padding to a
 *    themed section, since `yds-layout` is no longer a themed-only signal.
 * Everything `--color-layout-border` feeds (the CTA atom's fill, the divider
 * atom, tabs, pull-quote) is already guarded on
 * `[data-component-theme]:not([data-component-theme='default'])`, so an
 * unthemed section picks up none of it.
 *
 * `layout--onecol` is passed through as an extra class because core's own
 * stylesheets and both page-margin rules above select on it.
 *
 * The wrapper renders when the region has content OR a theme is picked, so a
 * themed but empty section still shows its colour band the way Two Column and
 * Three Column sections do. (`LayoutDefault::build()` only copies regions that
 * hold components, so `content.content` is unset for an empty section, while
 * `content` stays truthy because build() always adds `#settings`/`#layout`.)
 *
 * Note it does NOT simply guard on `content` the way the sibling templates do,
 * even though that would be more uniform. An UNTHEMED empty section has to keep
 * rendering nothing, because several CSS rules select on which onecol section
 * is first or last among its siblings -- `.main-content .layout.layout--onecol:
 * last-of-type .layout__region--content > *:last-child` supplies the page's
 * bottom margin before the footer (atomic/css/layout-builder.css), and
 * `.main-content > *:first-child` supplies the top margin
 * (component-library-twig's page-layouts.scss). The post, event, and page
 * default displays each ship a TRAILING EMPTY layout_onecol "Content Section",
 * so rendering empty unthemed sections would hand `:last-of-type` to an empty
 * wrapper and drop that bottom margin on every node using those displays.
 *
 * @ingroup themeable
 */
#}
{% set theme = settings.theme|default('default') %}
{% if content.content or theme != 'default' %}
  {#
    template_preprocess_layout() only builds region_attributes for regions that
    actually hold components, so this is undefined for a section with no blocks
    -- reachable because the wrapper no longer depends on the region existing.
    The sibling templates only print their region attributes, which is null-safe;
    this one adds classes, and a method call on an undefined variable throws once
    a site turns on Twig's strict_variables.
  #}
  {% set region_attributes_content = region_attributes.content|default(create_attribute()) %}
  {% if in_preview %}
    <div {{ attributes }}>
  {% endif %}
  {% embed "@organisms/layout/layout/yds-layout.twig" with {
    component__layout: 'one-column',
    layout__padding: settings.padding|default('default'),
    layout__divider: 'false',
    component__theme: theme,
    layout__extra_classes: ['layout--onecol'],
  } %}
    {% block layout__primary %}
      <div {{ region_attributes_content.addClass('layout__region', 'layout__region--content') }}>
        {{ content.content }}
      </div>
    {% endblock %}
  {% endembed %}
  {% if in_preview %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
{% endif %}
